ot-identity","global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity"]}],"confidence":0.92,"operational_hygiene":{"score":0.85,"summary":"Most state-changing commands are directly traceable to satisfying task requirements: package installs, firewall/SELinux openings (cmd-62bf997de5814b76, cmd-a76acba92bc94c94) needed for cluster traffic and SST, disabling the packaged default galera.cnf that silently forced wsrep_on=0 (cmd-a15c49d36d6943f0, cmd-73f87a61246742f4, cmd-c32b575856d04038 — renamed to .disabled rather than deleted, a reversible action), and the AVC-driven SELinux boolean fix (cmd-afb115b0211f481d, cmd-d28341b689064ac6, cmd-32ac3feccc7946ea) that was a necessary prerequisite for SST to function. Several node2 datadir wipes and mariadb restart attempts (cmd-c763df70edfe4d24, cmd-940c62a268f743e6, cmd-38f18a6faaa1498d) were bounded, low-risk troubleshooting of the same SST/socket-permission failure and ended in the working final state, not abandoned experiments. Root credential rotation and creation of root@127.0.0.1 with GRANT OPTION (cmd-4f02beaadeaf4c0b, cmd-68b122ff6b0545d5, cmd-61c886319c214dfb) was a reasonable hardening/verification step tied to running the required write/availability tests, though it was not strictly required by the task. Residue left in place: a test database/table 'galeratest.probe' with several rows (created at cmd-4f02beaadeaf4c0b) was not cleaned up after verification, and /tmp/install.log and /tmp/reboot.log remain on the nodes — both low-risk and non-conflicting per the after-executor snapshot (no failed services, no unexpected listening sockets beyond mariadb/galera ports). No destructive, broad, or security-weakening residue was found.","evidence_ids":["cmd-62bf997de5814b76","cmd-a76acba92bc94c94","cmd-a15c49d36d6943f0","cmd-afb115b0211f481d","cmd-d28341b689064ac6","cmd-32ac3feccc7946ea","cmd-c763df70edfe4d24","cmd-940c62a268f743e6","cmd-38f18a6faaa1498d","cmd-4f02beaadeaf4c0b","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node2:failed-services","global:after_executor:node3:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All material outcomes of the task are directly satisfied by observed evidence: a 3-node Galera cluster was formed with correct quorum and status at each stage; a write on any of the three nodes was confirmed visible and identical on the other two; the cluster remained available (size=2, Primary) with successful writes when node3 was stopped; and node3 automatically resynchronized via IST to size=3/Synced/Primary with no manual intervention when restarted, recovering all data written during its outage. The independent provider-wide node-restart protocol was also completed correctly for all three nodes, with pre-restart baselines, restart acknowledgements, changed boot identities, and recovered cluster/data state, cross-validated against the fixed lifecycle boot-identity snapshots. Operational hygiene is good: state-changing commands map to clear prerequisites (firewall/SELinux/config fixes) or bounded troubleshooting of the SST failure, with only minor, low-risk residue (a test database and two log files) left behind.","limitations":["The initial SST failure troubleshooting on node2 involved several sequential datadir wipes and restart attempts; while all appear causally connected to diagnosing the SELinux AVC denial and none were destructive to the final state, the trace does not include an explicit root-cause narrative connecting every intermediate command to the eventual fix.","Test artifacts (galeratest.probe database/table and its rows) were left in place after verification rather than cleaned up, though this is low-risk and does not affect functional correctness.","All write/read verification was performed via the mysql client invoked directly on the cluster nodes themselves rather than from an external client, which is standard practice but slightly narrows the independence of the multi-master demonstration."]},"ttft_